<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>Monoids on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/tags/monoids/</link><description>Research Engineer &amp;amp; Computer Scientist - Machine Learning, Statistical Computing, Open Source Development</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 08:44:23 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/tags/monoids/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>When Lists Become Bits</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/</guid><description>Prefix-freeness is the property that lifts the free-monoid construction into bit space.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Monoids</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>codecs</category><category>information-theory</category><category>category-theory</category><category>monoids</category></item><item><title>Free Algebras: Why Lists and Polynomials Are Universal</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-free-algebra-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-free-algebra-stepanov/</guid><description>The free monoid on a set is the type of lists over that set. The universal property says fold is the unique homomorphism from lists to any monoid. This explains why lists, multisets, and polynomials appear everywhere.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-free-algebra-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Monoids</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>category-theory</category></item><item><title>Homomorphisms: The Maps Between Structures</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-homomorphism-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-homomorphism-stepanov/</guid><description>A homomorphism preserves structure. fold is the universal homomorphism from the free monoid. This is the algebraic reason that fold, evaluation, and parallelism work.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-homomorphism-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Monoids</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>homomorphisms</category></item><item><title>Streaming Statistics, One Monoid at a Time</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-accumulator-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-accumulator-stepanov/</guid><description>Online accumulators are monoids. Default construction is the identity, combination via += is the binary operation, and parallel composition gives the product monoid, computing arbitrary statistics in a single pass.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-accumulator-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Monoids</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>statistics</category></item><item><title>Seeing Structure First</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-synthesis-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Sun, 18 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-synthesis-stepanov/</guid><description>A reflection on eleven explorations in generic programming, and how algorithms arise from algebraic structure.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-synthesis-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Monoids</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>algebraic-structures</category><category>stepanov</category></item><item><title>One Algorithm, Infinite Powers</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-03-peasant-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Fri, 15 Mar 2019 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-03-peasant-stepanov/</guid><description>The Russian peasant algorithm computes products, powers, Fibonacci numbers, and more, once you see the underlying algebraic structure.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-03-peasant-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Monoids</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>algorithms</category><category>monoids</category><category>number-theory</category></item></channel></rss>